  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Areas of dense fog possible again tonight \n(Monday night). Any fog would impact travel conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION... Areas of fog appear likely again \ntonight thanks to light winds and a decent amount of low-level \nmoisture. There may be a slightly later onset to the fog in the \nevening this evening compared to last night as we have a bit of \nbreeze this afternoon that still needs to come down, but still \nthink the fog could be locally dense by later tonight. The best \nchance of dense fog is near the Downeast coast, though dense fog\nis possible anywhere.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Chance of showers and thunderstorms from Bangor\nnorth Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... More showers and storms could \noccur Tuesday afternoon for everywhere except right near the \ncoast, with a 30-50 percent chance. With colder air aloft and an\nupper trough moving through, some of the storms could have some\nsmall hail. Not expecting anything severe though.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Thunderstorms are possible Friday afternoon and\nevening, some of which may be strong with gusty winds and small\nhail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION... A cold front crosses the area \nFriday afternoon and evening on the leading edge of a full \nlatitude trough pushing into the region, with the added support \nof a northern stream shortwave pushing through the trough around\nthat time. With the potential for 500-1500 J/kg of instability \nand 25-35KT of bulk shear, there is a risk of some locally \nstrong thunderstorms ahead of the front, with the main threat \nexpected to be strong gusty winds. The greatest chance for \nstorms is likely to be concentrated over northern and central \nareas while more southern areas are likely to remain too stable \ndue to southerly flow.",
